#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199027} :activity/id #uuid "bd6ad418-e22b-5009-b330-e1172a694c56" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050536 :topic/id #uuid "51d6809f-cbef-5f23-be22-3c2d79930c37" :topic/math-academy-id 4218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199508 :tutorial/content "We can use differentiation to find the shortest distance between a point and a curve. \n\nFor example, let's find the smallest distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+1$ and the point $(5,1),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1f5b350025a0fd0530531cfc887c148459194afe209e472701054c128f1079.png)\n\nTo solve this problem, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1.** Use the distance formula to create a function $S(x,y)$ representing the *squared* distance between $(5,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\nS = (x-5)^2 + (y-1)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Calculate the first derivative $S'(x)$ and solve $S'(x) = 0$ to find the stationary point(s).\n\nComputing the derivative $S'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where $S'(x) = 0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-8 & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Use the first or second derivative tests to confirm that the stationary point minimizes the squared distance.\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\nS''(4) = 2.\n$$\n\nSince $S''(4) > 0,$ the point $x=4$ is a minimum of $S(x)$ by the second derivative test.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x = 4.$ \n\nFinally, since $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ef45dd95-3bca-5734-a692-3cebc9f37432" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7893 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "25198a50-d410-5851-a1cc-00f60435537f" :step/math-academy-id 119264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199028}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199028 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198079 :question/id #uuid "39ffb646-57fa-5d6d-a81f-286948f66d83" :question/math-academy-id "e-12596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050952}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526097}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance"} :step/id #uuid "e07168c9-fa86-5a07-b2f0-b9f7aade6c3b" :step/math-academy-id 119265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199507 :tutorial/content "If the squared distance function has multiple stationary points, we must use the candidates test for *global* extrema to identify which stationary point corresponds to the smallest distance.\n\nTo do that, we evaluate the squared distance at each stationary point and compare the results.\n\nMultiple critical points may correspond to the same squared distance. In such cases, like that shown in the graph below, there would be multiple points of minimum distance.\n\n![](images/de/dea83b2804a816eef505810237940e2d9bb8547ffe77549f833ac04823fa6d30.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c98fb0ba-d8a2-5069-b3c5-04961452caa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7889 :tutorial/title "The Candidates Test For Multiple Stationary Points"} :step/id #uuid "eff95439-12b9-5276-a8f1-b3b4ee7e96f1" :step/math-academy-id 119268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199030}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199030 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198247 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198080 :question/id #uuid "4b55bd03-c8ad-55b2-a51e-7f875d382627" :question/math-academy-id "e-12597"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547033}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point"} :step/id #uuid "97876c5f-6760-51ef-a427-274559dccac4" :step/math-academy-id 119269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199031}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198248 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198083 :question/id #uuid "2bf999cc-9733-5f29-90e6-20d297efe352" :question/math-academy-id "e-12606"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547054}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve"} :step/id #uuid "798c8ee2-b4c9-57b8-b340-831e2641810f" :step/math-academy-id 119272}] :activity/title "Optimizing Distances to Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198506}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547157} :activity/id #uuid "49557175-272f-5590-8a63-95f82b2b3ff6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050536 :topic/id #uuid "51d6809f-cbef-5f23-be22-3c2d79930c37" :topic/math-academy-id 4218} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2c19332-1daa-53e4-8c7e-f6edf5736887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546716} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9413410-1a9d-50b7-9182-e546f0368ec5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a9dfae3-b740-55e6-abd9-d9909c3ad491" :question/math-academy-id "q-344835" :question/problem "![](images/48/48b2ec3916610f624aa308d9c395c43eb7a34418a6457c514dc40ecc82bf2f99.png)\n\nFind the least distance $d$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}+1$ and the point $(2,1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,1)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4x-4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=4>0,$ so $x=1$ gives a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(1)^{2}-4(1)+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "14d7851b-2592-5b3a-96a9-8d51149c8a93" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54db5f0b-c4e1-5e3f-bd51-fea3d81ff682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546437} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77e5f19-97fb-5309-b828-6d59517070d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5957844f-5ee9-5592-b52f-3fea8df780b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-315639" :question/problem "![](images/83/835ffbb783651cfc8f6210fe46f792d7c68f41926a4775e56a570e9bb87b7462.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=\\sqrt{9x+1}$ that is closest to the point $(8,0).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(8,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\nS=(x-8)^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{9x+1}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-8)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-8)^{2}+(\\sqrt{9x+1})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-8)^{2}+9x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-16x+64+9x+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-7x+65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-7 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "24b7c382-5fad-5c82-8a7d-642129b1c47a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4c3d4e-5b73-5c34-a4c3-53c48c541b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546376} {:answer/id #uuid "72775d93-ea9b-54c6-9567-4c8e764952f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546504} {:answer/id #uuid "b641d9de-cddd-5173-aac6-c811671c0ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546659} {:answer/id #uuid "bb82afd5-4e30-5905-aad5-28701a369b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546672} {:answer/id #uuid "c96541f4-c7b4-562c-ab5d-50aefc222234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546699}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546699} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0295622-35a4-5953-aa14-52d047c70fc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "907519ae-3321-5175-a88f-6dce32f2cb03" :question/math-academy-id "q-18500" :question/problem "Find the $y$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x$ that is closest to the point $(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(1,4)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-8y+16 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-8y+17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =y+{y}^{2}-8y+17 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-7y+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(y)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y)=2y-7 & =0 \\\\ 2y & =7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(y)=2>0.$ Therefore, $y=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $y=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "2c5a3b83-ee27-5938-906f-66932bc34a39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbfe3030-34aa-5743-844c-793f65847a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546736} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae0782a8-a8ce-5695-a710-1fed57c8a4e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ceab1153-db5f-5530-aee8-9a4937774de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-344849" :question/problem "Find the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2$ and the point $P(2,2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$d=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(2,2)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{|x-1|}+2-2)}^{2} \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+|x-1|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1,$ we have $|x-1|=x-1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-2)^{2}+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+x-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-3(\\frac{3}{2})+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{9}{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a088826c-f52e-5e69-8491-0696adb96bbc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aef2d58-4f97-565f-92e2-f7a18012072e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197535} {:answer/id #uuid "9859e4bd-f0ea-5c7d-9135-59281613b137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197536} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfb6463-e116-5658-af37-8050c0e1de81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197537} {:answer/id #uuid "bfdbe21a-4020-5375-bc77-32e9f4bfc13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197538} {:answer/id #uuid "567ac564-b878-51eb-8aa3-e254ea745d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197535} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42b1007-56d3-5b64-871d-19d6670e6fc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f5ed0b17-cac6-5f50-8a04-2449a141ebc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-3442" :question/problem "![](images/72/720713e7e1dbfbc9ce3ebb486e8744327c633745d00df7dc862d794eefe57bbc.png)\n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $(4,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(4,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-7x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x-7 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(x)=2>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=\\frac{7}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S(\\frac{7}{2})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(\\frac{7}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-7(\\frac{7}{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db305256-c310-5e02-ade5-477388d7db01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547102}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Distances to Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546240}]}
